DISCUSSION BOARD — SELF-HOSTED MATRIX / ELEMENT
Design note — v0.1 — 2026-10-01
Status: DECIDED, NOT BUILT. Nothing has been installed.


================================================================================
1. THE REQUIREMENTS, AND HOW THEY GOT US HERE
================================================================================

Three requirements, in the order they were decided:

  * OPEN SOURCE
  * HOSTED IN THE UK OR THE EU, so the conversation is not subject to US
    jurisdiction
  * NO AGE-BASED MESSAGE DELETION

THE ROUTE TAKEN, worth keeping because each step looked reasonable:

  Slack was ruled out first and for the clearest reason: its free tier shows
  only the last 90 days and, by one account, deletes content older than a year.
  The retention requirement alone disqualifies it.

  Discord was chosen next, and looked strong on retention — it keeps history
  indefinitely on the free tier. It was rejected on jurisdiction. Discord, Inc.
  is a US company and "processes and stores information on servers located in
  the United States". There is no EU data residency option, and as a US
  provider it is exposed to the CLOUD Act: US legal process could compel access
  regardless of where data sits.

  That matters more than usual here because the portal has just been through a
  deliberate exercise in who can see what — sandboxes private to their owner, no
  admin read path, impersonation as the only way in. Putting the studio's
  conversation somewhere US process can reach would have undone a good deal of
  that thinking.

  A THIRD-PARTY EU/UK BOARD was considered next. Element is available hosted,
  from roughly EUR 5/user/month, with EU residency. That remains the fallback if
  self-hosting proves too much.

  SELF-HOSTING was then chosen, because it is the only option that satisfies all
  three requirements at once AND makes real integration possible — see section 4,
  which is the part that actually distinguishes it.


================================================================================
2. WHAT WE WOULD RUN
================================================================================

  Synapse          the Matrix homeserver — holds the rooms and the messages
  PostgreSQL       Synapse's database
  Element Web      the client, served as static files at e.g. chat.<domain>
  nginx            reverse proxy (already present in shell.nix)
  TURN server      only if voice/video must work through restrictive networks

Synapse speaks to clients over HTTPS; nginx fronts it. Element Web is just
static files pointing at the homeserver, so it can be served from the same host
or anywhere.


================================================================================
3. WHY SYNAPSE, WHEN IT IS THE HEAVIEST OF THE THREE
================================================================================

Normally the lighter homeserver would be the recommendation. Two things rule
that out, and one of them is decisive.

  Conduit (Rust, ~256 MB) is by far the lightest. Its lineage is a mess: the
  original lead stepped back, conduwuit was discontinued in 2025, and active
  development scattered across forks (continuwuity, tuwunel). Betting the
  studio's chat on that is not sensible.

  Dendrite (Go, ~512 MB) is in MAINTENANCE MODE — security patches only, no new
  features, and the feature gap with Synapse is no longer closing.

  THE DECISIVE POINT: the features this project needs for integration are
  exactly the ones the lighter servers do not have. Synapse is the only
  implementation with a complete admin API and OIDC/SSO support. Cheap to run
  and unable to integrate is the wrong trade here — section 4 is the whole
  reason this is worth self-hosting at all.

Also recorded, because it is a trap: you CANNOT reuse the same (sub)domain when
migrating a homeserver from Synapse to Dendrite — it breaks federation. Changing
homeserver later is not a drop-in swap.


================================================================================
4. INTEGRATION WITH THE PORTAL — THE REASON THIS IS WORTH DOING
================================================================================

A third-party board means a second identity system: separate accounts, roles
that do not carry over, and — the sharp edge — revoking portal access does NOT
revoke board access. The portal's own revocation is immediate, because
authenticateToken re-reads is_active on every request; Discord's was not.

Self-hosting lets that be fixed rather than documented. Three rungs, in
increasing order of value and effort:

  RUNG 1 — LINK OUT
    What exists today. frontend/discussion.html links to the board. Separate
    accounts, no connection. Costs nothing, solves nothing.

  RUNG 2 — ADMIN API SYNC          <- START HERE
    Synapse has an admin REST API. The portal calls it:

      portal account created      -> create the Matrix user
      portal account deactivated  -> DEACTIVATE the Matrix user

    The second line is the point. A revoked tester loses the board immediately,
    the same moment they lose the portal — which is the thing Discord could not
    offer and the reason this is worth the operational cost.

  RUNG 3 — SSO
    Synapse supports OIDC, so the portal could become the identity provider and
    one login would cover both. This closes the identity gap entirely: roles,
    invitations, deactivation, all from one account. Meaningfully more work —
    it means implementing an OIDC provider in the portal — and rung 2 does not
    have to be undone to get there.

A DEPENDENCY WORTH NOTING: the portal currently has NO way to deactivate an
account. authenticateToken reads is_active, but nothing writes it. So rung 2's
deactivation path has nothing to hook into yet — the portal needs a deactivate
feature before the sync has anything to propagate. That is small, and it is
worth doing on its own merits.


================================================================================
5. SIZING, COST, AND WHERE THE MACHINE GOES
================================================================================

For a studio of 10-50 people: Synapse with PostgreSQL on roughly 4 GB RAM and
2 cores. That is about EUR 5-10 per month, and there are no per-user fees at any
point.

Use PostgreSQL, not SQLite. Synapse's own documentation says SQLite is for
development only and "will become extremely slow" in production.

WHERE THE MACHINE IS MATTERS MORE THAN THE MACHINE. Self-hosting only solves
the jurisdiction problem if the host is in the UK or the EU. A US VPS would put
us straight back where Discord had us, and would be a worse position because it
would look private while being no different.


================================================================================
6. THE HONEST COST
================================================================================

This is now infrastructure the project runs: upgrades, backups, monitoring, a
domain, TLS certificates, and the assumption that someone will notice when it
breaks.

BACKUPS ARE NOT OPTIONAL. With no age-based deletion the database grows
forever, and chat history is exactly the thing that cannot be regenerated from
anything else. Losing it means losing it.

The trade, stated plainly: jurisdiction, open source, no per-user cost, and
genuine integration — paid for in operational attention. If that attention is
not available, hosted Element is the middle ground and should be taken
deliberately rather than drifted into.


================================================================================
7. OPEN QUESTIONS
================================================================================

  * WHICH MACHINE. Must be UK or EU. Decides the whole jurisdiction argument.
  * VOICE? Element Call needs more infrastructure than text does. If voice is
    dropped, the TURN server and a good deal of complexity go with it. The
    original specification did mention voice.
  * WHO ADMINISTERS IT. Who holds the Synapse admin token, and who can
    deactivate a Matrix user directly rather than through the portal.
  * ROOM STRUCTURE. Channels, and whether testers and developers get different
    spaces. This is the point where roles would have to mean something on the
    board, if they ever do.
  * RETENTION, AGAIN, EXPLICITLY. "No age-based deletion" is a requirement
    here, and with self-hosting it is a config decision rather than a vendor's
    promise. Set it deliberately and write it down.
  * WHETHER RUNG 3 IS EVER NEEDED. If rung 2 turns out to be enough, SSO is a
    large piece of work for a smaller gain than it first appears.


================================================================================
8. WHEN IT IS BUILT
================================================================================

  1. Provision a UK/EU machine. Set Synapse's retention explicitly.
  2. Stand up Synapse + PostgreSQL + Element Web behind nginx.
  3. Set VITE_DISCUSSION_URL to the Element Web address. One line — the
     discussion page is already service-neutral and takes its URL from config.
  4. Add portal-side deactivation, which is needed regardless of the board.
  5. Implement rung 2: create on registration, deactivate on deactivation.
  6. Consider rung 3 separately, later.

Nothing in the portal blocks on this. The page says "not configured" until
VITE_DISCUSSION_URL is set, which is the correct behaviour and not an error.
